WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.888
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.855 --> 00:00:07.480
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
I'm Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.014
I'm a Product Manager in
container security at Google.

00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:11.180
JESSE ENDAHL: Hi, I'm Jesse.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.990
I'm the Chief Product Officer
and Chief Security Officer

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.610
at a startup called Fleetsmith.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.070
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: You have
a lot of titles, Jesse.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.740
Feel free to tweet at us
later or during the talk.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.560
So we'll be talking first about
what container security is.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.297
So why containers
and why Kubernetes?

00:00:25.297 --> 00:00:27.880
Well, Kubernetes makes a lot of
constructs a little bit easier

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:28.870
for you to manage--

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.760
things like scheduling,
so to decide

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.439
where your containers
get deployed,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:34.290
lifecycle management,
how many containers

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.080
you should have up and
running, scaling to bring up

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.637
and size down sets of
containers, and load balancing,

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:42.970
so you can send traffic to
the right sets of containers.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.634
But Kubernetes also has a bunch
of security features built in--

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.050
things like logging
and monitoring

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.190
to make it easier
for you to figure out

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.580
what's happening
in your container.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.413
This is not like a log
aggregation en masse,

00:00:51.413 --> 00:00:54.072
but gives you a lot more
than you would get otherwise.

00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:55.780
Debugging and
introspection to figure out

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.790
what's happening
inside your containers

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.610
or to attach to your containers,
and identity and authorization

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.795
to determine who can do
what with your containers.

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:05.170
And more and more
of these things

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.640
are being added to Kubernetes
as part of every release.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.930
So you're already getting a lot
of security features built in.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.970
Running containers,
though, as a concept,

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.430
is a slightly different security
model than you're used to.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.860
It gives you a fundamentally
different set of benefits.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.312
So containers are short lived
and frequently redeployed.

00:01:22.312 --> 00:01:24.770
That means you can be constantly
patching your environment.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.300
Things that you were
doing passively before,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.880
like patching your
OS, where you had

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.020
to take down Sunday, 2:00
AM maintenance windows,

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.228
you don't have to do anymore.

00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:33.980
You can just patch that image
and then redeploy it passively

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.680
when a new container comes
up in your environment.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.670
Containers are also
immutable, so you

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.670
can control what's deployed
in your environment.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.350
By having these short
lived containers,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.800
you have a single
enforcement point--

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.230
a single choke point
for everything that

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.396
ends up in your environment.

00:01:46.396 --> 00:01:48.360
It's much easier to
figure out, for example,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.318
if you're subject to a
particular vulnerability

00:01:50.318 --> 00:01:52.610
because you know
what you've deployed.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.830
A common reason people
don't want to use containers

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:55.967
for security is isolation.

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:58.550
You know, they say, hey, I have
this thing called a hypervisor

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.770
that I know really well
with a virtual machine

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.486
and I know it's really secure
and isolates my workload.

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:05.360
And until recently,
until things like gVisor,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.556
we didn't have something
similar with containers.

00:02:07.556 --> 00:02:08.930
So now we do, and
that's actually

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.013
one of the greatest strengths
to using containers,

00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:12.620
from a security point of view.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.900
And lastly, a lot of
good security defaults

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.020
in containers in Kubernetes
are one line changes.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.390
You can follow best practices
like enabling seccomp

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.740
without taking on a lot
of the work yourself.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.730
So it's really-- containers
allow you to think completely

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.060
differently about how you can
patch and manage your system,

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.240
compared to how you're doing
it with virtual machines.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.027
So your mindset's a little bit
different, but a lot of things

00:02:35.027 --> 00:02:35.860
are the same, right?

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.360
If you had an
identity management

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.860
system with your VMs, you have
an identity management system

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:40.930
with your containers.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.540
If you had a network management
system-- a network security

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.760
solution, you have a network
security solution here.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.290
You still want firewalls, right?

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.210
You still want an
IDS IPS, et cetera.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.110
That stuff doesn't change.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.182
So what does actually change?

00:02:53.182 --> 00:02:54.890
A few things, which
we'll point out here.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:56.950
The first is the
surface of attack.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.110
So your container doesn't
have this hypervisor,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.818
but it does have this
host OS that you're

00:03:00.818 --> 00:03:03.370
running your containers
on, and the container

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.770
itself has its own OS
bundled with libraries

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:06.910
and dependencies.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.874
That host OS is supposed
to be really small.

00:03:08.874 --> 00:03:11.290
It's supposed to be something
that you can run everywhere,

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.660
that it's really easy for
you to patch and manage.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.243
So if you're doing
containers properly

00:03:15.243 --> 00:03:17.950
you're thinking about securing
that host OS really tightly

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.530
and patching it and
keeping it minimal.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.210
Resource isolation--
containers have

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.520
concepts for resource
isolation built in,

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.410
like namespaces
and cgroups, so you

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.170
can use those natively
to do that without having

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.720
to bring in other capabilities.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.486
Permission management--
so access controls

00:03:33.486 --> 00:03:35.110
for app privileges
and shared resources

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.299
are built into containers.

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:37.840
But keep in mind
that containers have

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.276
access to a wider
range of sys calls

00:03:40.276 --> 00:03:41.650
than a traditional
process would,

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.120
so it potentially is
much more powerful.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.250
And then lastly, lifetime.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.830
Containers have a shorter
average of lifetime.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.410
In fact, systeg estimates
that 95% of containers

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.130
live less than a week.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.900
What that means is
that an attacker who

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.009
gets into your environment--
she can't persist there much

00:03:59.009 --> 00:03:59.800
longer than a week.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.530
It's much harder for someone to
stay there without you knowing.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.905
That being said, by the time
you figure out something bad

00:04:04.905 --> 00:04:07.312
has gone on, the attacker
might not be there anymore.

00:04:07.312 --> 00:04:09.020
The container might
not be there anymore.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.395
You might not be able to do
forensics and figure out what

00:04:11.395 --> 00:04:12.697
happened in your environment.

00:04:12.697 --> 00:04:15.280
So this brings us a little bit
to how we think about container

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.666
security at Google.

00:04:16.666 --> 00:04:18.790
Some of the different kinds
of threats that we see.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.700
So things like-- the
first are the attacks

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.250
to Kubernetes peripherals--
things like credential theft,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.460
privilege escalation, the
API compromise, et cetera.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.160
These were things around how
you configure Kubernetes--

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.140
configure environment.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.070
The next one are software
supply chain vulnerabilities--

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.850
unpatched vulnerability,
a new 0 day that

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.610
drops in a common library, an
issue with open source library

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.080
that you're using, et cetera.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.663
And lastly, we have
things that really

00:04:42.663 --> 00:04:44.530
scare people-- things
like DDoS attacks,

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.950
container escapes, flooding
your event pipeline,

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.670
compromising the nodes that
you're running on, et cetera.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.560
And so we've grouped these
into three different areas--

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.930
infrastructure security,
software supply chain,

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.061
and container runtime security.

00:04:58.061 --> 00:04:59.560
Infrastructure
security is about how

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.300
you secure the development
of your containers.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.010
How do I do secret management,
networking, access management,

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.024
IAM, all of those things to
make sure that the environment

00:05:09.024 --> 00:05:11.440
that I'm developing-- that
Kubernetes or Kubernetes engine

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:12.600
is secure?

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.350
Software supply chain
is about making sure

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.797
that the images that I deploy
and the binaries that I use

00:05:16.797 --> 00:05:19.130
are secure for building and
deploying in my environment.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.470
How do I know that they're
free of vulnerabilities?

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.210
How do I know that
they came out of my

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.550
build pipeline, et cetera?

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.620
And container runtime
security is making sure

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.240
that my container
is secure to run.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.550
It's my traditional
sec ops team--

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.050
the people in the
[? sock ?] who are

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.300
going to have to sit there
and respond to an incident.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.440
What can they get?

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.580
How do they know what's
happening in my container?

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.204
How do they do
forensics after the fact

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:41.630
and make sure that we've
gotten rid of that threat?

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.450
So the question you're
probably asking yourselves

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.590
and a lot of people
ask themselves,

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.339
and sometimes I ask
myself when I wake up,

00:05:50.339 --> 00:05:52.690
is how do I secure Kubernetes?

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.072
It's this massive thing that
nobody really understands yet,

00:05:56.072 --> 00:05:56.780
and that's right.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.238
Lots of people
don't understand it.

00:05:58.238 --> 00:06:00.050
It's so new that a
lot of people don't

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.730
know what the security controls
that come with Kubernetes are.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.610
So that's what we're really
trying to do today-- teach you

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.651
about some of the things
that exist in Kubernetes

00:06:06.651 --> 00:06:09.117
and in Kubernetes' engine to
help secure your environment.

00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:10.700
JESSE ENDAHL: So
it's really important

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.830
to remember that
Kubernetes is only

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.160
the sort of middle slice
in a greater picture,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.800
and so you don't want to forget
about securing your application

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.980
layer, your containers, whatever
your container runtime is--

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.390
maybe that's Docker.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:27.450
Your virtual machines-- they're
actually running sort of below,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.930
but today, we're obviously
talking about the middle layer

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:30.889
of Kubernetes itself.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:33.180
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah, what
are some of the components

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.460
that we have?

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.140
JESSE ENDAHL: Yeah, so there's
really only three major kind

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:38.640
of pieces.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.930
First, you've got the
sort of storage layer.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.230
You could think of that
like Kubernetes' hard drive.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.960
That's where all of your
cluster state is going to live.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.364
Next up, you've got your master.

00:06:47.364 --> 00:06:48.780
That's what's
actually controlling

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.520
your entire cluster.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.710
Next, of course,
you have the nodes.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.140
That's basically going
to be virtual machines,

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.870
and that's where your workloads
are actually deployed.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.760
So that's where your
pods are deployed,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.300
and pods are really just
collections of containers.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.606
So now we can go ahead
and walk through some

00:07:05.606 --> 00:07:07.730
of the different things
that could kind of go wrong

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.850
at each of these layers.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:14.260
So starting from the top
down, if an attacker controls

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.400
a container, what's the
worst case scenario?

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.320
The worst case scenario
is that they escape

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.900
and they're able to
attack the node, right?

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.330
If they attack the
node, they can also

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.460
control all the pods
running on that note,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.160
and then potentially
attack the next layer up--

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.240
the master.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.450
Or they could abuse compute,
maybe, to mine cryptocurrency,

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.540
for example.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:34.400
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Affect
the other workloads

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.500
that you have running so
that something else that's

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.900
really critical might not be
able to run as you wanted it

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:39.824
to.

00:07:39.824 --> 00:07:41.240
JESSE ENDAHL: And
then, of course,

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.823
if they can control
the storage layer,

00:07:42.823 --> 00:07:47.192
etcd, that means they can
create, modify, or destroy

00:07:47.192 --> 00:07:49.400
any state in your cluster,
very similar to if someone

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.639
yanked out a hard
drive from a computer.

00:07:51.639 --> 00:07:52.430
It's not encrypted.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:55.100
They can just modify
whatever data they want to.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.280
So obviously, that's
pretty bad, as well.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.530
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah, this
is a super helpful slide.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:01.410
Take a photo.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.300
This is the kind of
thing that, when I first

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.850
started looking into Kubernetes
security, didn't exist.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.190
And it was hard to
explain to people

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.960
why you had to
protect etcd or why

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.340
you had to protect the master.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.760
So I'll often seen people say,
hey, I'll put one key in etcd

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.150
and I'll put one key on the
pod, and it will all be safe.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.114
And it's like, what's your
lowest common denominator?

00:08:21.114 --> 00:08:22.530
And getting to
something like etcd

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.320
is pretty bad, so make
sure something-- etcd is

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:26.939
pretty locked down.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:27.980
JESSE ENDAHL: Absolutely.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:28.938
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure.

00:08:28.938 --> 00:08:31.137
Let's jump into
security requirements.

00:08:31.137 --> 00:08:33.470
So typically, when we're
having these kind of Kubernetes

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.450
security one on
one conversations,

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.700
there's two different
tacts that come along.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.117
One is a set of users who just
want to have basic security

00:08:40.117 --> 00:08:41.866
hygiene-- make sure
that they're not doing

00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:43.340
anything fundamentally wrong.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.173
They might have some
compliance requirements

00:08:45.173 --> 00:08:47.170
that they're trying to meet.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.670
If you're just worried about
this kind stuff-- making sure

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.997
you're not doing anything
particularly unusual,

00:08:52.997 --> 00:08:54.080
then this talk is for you.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.894
This is the basics in
that security domain.

00:08:56.894 --> 00:08:58.310
The second set of
users are people

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.190
who might be running a product
in a more high risk industry,

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.697
and more likely to
be subject to attack.

00:09:03.697 --> 00:09:05.780
That doesn't mean, of
course, that they don't also

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:06.946
have compliance regulations.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:10.310
It just maybe is not the same
way of thinking about it.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.214
And for them, this is
a good starting point,

00:09:12.214 --> 00:09:14.630
but we'll put a link at the
end of the talk that leads you

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.880
to more hardening advice.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.050
So how do we know
anything about this topic?

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.150
I used to be a management
consultant doing work

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.190
with financial, health
care, and insurance

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.490
companies in security, and
Jesse here leads Fleetsmith.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:29.960
Tell us about that.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.390
JESSE ENDAHL: Yeah, so because
of what our product does,

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.960
we have a really kind of
high bar for security.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:39.090
And what our product does is it
automates Apple device setup,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.770
including the OS updates,
app installations, security

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:45.140
settings, things
related to compliance.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:46.850
And so concretely,
what that means

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:50.750
is buy a laptop from Apple,
drop ship it to an employee.

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.330
It boots up and it configures
itself automatically.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:55.550
So all of the important
apps can install--

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.180
Google Chrome, Dropbox Slack,
your VPN disk encryption

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:00.110
gets enforced.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:01.580
All that stuff, right?

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.470
But obviously, because of
all that functionality,

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.580
we have a need for a
strong security posture,

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.610
so that's one of the reasons we
chose Google Cloud is because

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.920
of their approach to security.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.090
And then we also do some
things on top of that.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.960
So we run our own Kubernetes
deployment on top of GCE,

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.190
and then we secure it with
certs issued from Vault--

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:23.420
HashiCorp Vault.
Awesome project.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.550
I think there's a
talk later on, yeah.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:31.162
And so all that's to say
that's where I'm coming from,

00:10:31.162 --> 00:10:32.120
in terms of background.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:33.950
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So
wait, your product

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.600
lets me use G Suite
to authenticate,

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.070
and then you guys run on GCP?

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:37.270
JESSE ENDAHL: Indeed.

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:38.980
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It's like you
guys love Google or something.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:39.530
It's great.

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:40.370
JESSE ENDAHL: We do.

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.190
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: All right.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:43.940
So let's talk about
the variety of things

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.190
you might want to do in
your security journey

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:49.562
to getting to a secure
Kubernetes deployment.

00:10:49.562 --> 00:10:51.395
There's a whole lot of
things on this slide.

00:10:51.395 --> 00:10:52.790
Do you want to talk us
through some of these?

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.790
JESSE ENDAHL: Yeah, so
obviously the first thing

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.020
you need to do,
especially a lot of these,

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.630
if you're running
your own cluster,

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.020
is sort of the security
of the cluster itself.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:08.120
And so that's things like
restricting access to kubectl,

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.140
setting up and using RBAC for
role-based access control,

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:15.440
using a network policy, setting
up namespaces, and of course,

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.740
bootstrapping TLS,
which requires

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.700
that you have a
CA to issue certs

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.466
to your entire
Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:21.466 --> 00:11:22.590
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Next up--

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:23.870
following basic
security hygiene.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.910
Making sure you can use your
containers for that security

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:27.710
benefit we were
talking about earlier--

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.410
keeping Kubernetes and
your OS and your images

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.840
and your binaries-- everything
patched and updated.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:34.520
Using a minimal
OS like container

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.310
optimized OS or COS on GKE.

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:40.790
Using minimum IAM roles to
enforce lease privilege.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.910
Using private IPs on your nodes
to make sure that nothing has

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.190
access from the public
internet that it shouldn't.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:47.930
Monitoring access
with auto logging

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:49.513
and then making sure
that the binaries

00:11:49.513 --> 00:11:51.290
that you deploy are
those that you build.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:51.830
JESSE ENDAHL: Next
up, you're going

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:53.990
to want to prevent
known attacks,

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:57.080
and so this is going to be
things like disabling the built

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:58.820
in Kubernetes
dashboard, if that's

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.360
running in your environment,
disabling the default surface

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:03.091
account token.

00:12:03.091 --> 00:12:05.090
That's something that
gets mounted automatically

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.830
into every new pod
in your cluster,

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:12.090
and it has some strong
permissions or privileges.

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.090
Next is protecting
node metadata,

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.347
and finally, scanning
images for known vulns.

00:12:17.347 --> 00:12:19.680
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And in the
final step of your maturity

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:22.047
security journey here,
preventing the impact

00:12:22.047 --> 00:12:23.130
of a potential compromise.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.020
So security is great.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:26.700
Shit happens.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.262
Humans make errors.

00:12:28.262 --> 00:12:29.470
What if something goes wrong?

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.590
You want to have multiple
layers of security-- things

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.423
like setting a pod
security policy to follow

00:12:33.423 --> 00:12:36.090
best practices like seccomp,
protecting your secrets using

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:39.000
things like encryption,
considering using sandboxing

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.869
to protect workloads
that need to be isolated,

00:12:41.869 --> 00:12:44.160
limiting the identity that
pods can use to authenticate

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.974
to other parts of
your infrastructure,

00:12:45.974 --> 00:12:48.390
and using a service mesh for
authentication and encryption

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.306
between your services.

00:12:52.384 --> 00:12:54.300
JESSE ENDAHL: So obviously,
we don't have time

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.790
to talk about everything
on this list today,

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:00.630
and so what we've
optimized for is six things

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:04.560
you can do that are high
impact and relatively low time

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.750
investment that
should significantly

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:08.735
increase your security posture.

00:13:08.735 --> 00:13:10.610
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Let's
go through those six.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:12.910
JESSE ENDAHL: So
first up, we have

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:14.895
restricting access to kubectl.

00:13:14.895 --> 00:13:16.200
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: kubectl.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.264
JESSE ENDAHL: kubectl.

00:13:18.264 --> 00:13:19.680
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Next up, using--

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:20.180
sorry.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:21.346
JESSE ENDAHL: No, that's it.

00:13:21.346 --> 00:13:21.850
Yeah.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.440
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Using
RBAC, our role-based access

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.550
control to define roles.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.800
It's basically Kubernetes'
native identity and access

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:27.975
management.

00:13:27.975 --> 00:13:30.100
JESSE ENDAHL: Next up is
defining a network policy,

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:33.530
and so by default, all pod
to pod traffic is open.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.260
And so if you don't
like that and you

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:37.570
want to increase
your security there,

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:39.580
you'd want to use
a network policy.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:41.080
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Protecting
the Kubernetes dashboard.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:43.246
You might have seen some
high profile hacks recently

00:13:43.246 --> 00:13:45.010
that take advantage
of this UI, so you

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:46.843
have a couple of options,
but the point is--

00:13:46.843 --> 00:13:48.130
don't leave it open.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:50.630
JESSE ENDAHL: Next is disabling
that default service account

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:53.570
token I mentioned because if
an attacker gets access to it,

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:55.420
they could use it
to do bad things.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.680
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And then
using pod security policy

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:58.510
to follow best
practices like seccomp

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.250
for protecting your containers.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:02.840
So let us jump in.

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:03.830
Oh yes.

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:06.009
As we talk through
this, Jesse will

00:14:06.009 --> 00:14:07.550
be talking about
Kubernetes, since he

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:09.900
has all this experience
deploying Kubernetes on Compute

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.320
Engine, and I'll be talking
about Kubernetes engine

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.424
and how we've deployed
those things there.

00:14:14.424 --> 00:14:17.090
Something to keep in mind around
the shared responsibility model

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:18.920
here is that
Kubernetes engine takes

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:21.014
care of the master for
you, including etcd,

00:14:21.014 --> 00:14:22.430
so you have some
options, in terms

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:24.847
of configuration of certain
features and things like that.

00:14:24.847 --> 00:14:26.346
But they're not
your responsibility.

00:14:26.346 --> 00:14:28.400
They're Google's
responsibility to protect,

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.650
whereas the nodes,
containers, and pods are still

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:33.234
your responsibility to
protect the Kubernetes engine.

00:14:33.234 --> 00:14:34.650
And maybe at the
end of this talk,

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:36.483
I'll have finally
convinced you to move over

00:14:36.483 --> 00:14:37.940
to Kubernetes engine.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.700
All right.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.210
JESSE ENDAHL: So number one,
restricting access to kubectl.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.500
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: kubectl.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:45.416
JESSE ENDAHL: kubectl.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:51.370
So the reason you
want to do this

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:55.390
is because kubectl is a very
powerful command line tool.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:59.170
If any of you have used the G
Cloud command line tools or AWS

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:00.910
command line tools,
this is very similar,

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:03.370
but it's specifically
for Kubernetes itself.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:04.990
And so obviously,
this lets you do

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:07.630
things like create, update,
and delete resources

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.840
within Kubernetes,
schedule pods--

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:11.740
pretty much anything.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:15.790
And so it's really important
to restrict not only

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.510
who can run this, but
where they can run it from.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.860
And so the way that
you can do that on GCE

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.630
is through firewall
rules and enforcing

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.210
kind of a bastion model.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:26.650
And so if anyone's
not familiar, that's

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:28.420
where you would
have a dedicated VM

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:31.120
or choke point where
you say the only way

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.550
to connect with
SSH into production

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.290
is through that one
host, and then you

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:38.140
can create firewall
rules that restrict

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.870
where you can call the
Kubernetes API from to just

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:41.620
your bastion host.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:43.161
And so that's what
we're seeing here.

00:15:43.161 --> 00:15:44.800
Highlighted in blue,
you see that we're

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.650
restricting the source
tags to the SSH bastion,

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:50.350
the target tags to the
Kubernetes controller,

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:55.240
and you would just apply this
through Deployment Manager,

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:55.970
in this case.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:56.920
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So just
follow the blue things

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:57.895
in the YAML file?

00:15:57.895 --> 00:15:58.770
JESSE ENDAHL: Indeed.

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.150
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Great.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.020
For Kubernetes engine,
we have a feature

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:03.330
called authorized networks.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.729
Authorized networks
lets you-- so

00:16:04.729 --> 00:16:07.020
first of all, when you connect
to the Kubernetes engine

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:09.880
master, that connection
is protected using TLS

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:11.530
and is authenticated.

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.530
And you can further restrict
the set of IP addresses

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:16.600
that can talk to the
Kubernetes master

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.520
using authorized networks.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.860
Authorized networks lets you
restrict up to 20 networks

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:25.640
that you'd like to
connect to using HTTPS.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:29.740
Kubernetes engine-- this
feature is currently in beta.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:32.260
Another feature that's related
to this is private clusters.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:33.460
That is also in beta.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:36.250
Private clusters gives
your nodes private RFC 1918

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.380
addresses to communicate
with the master

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.682
with and to use, anyways.

00:16:39.682 --> 00:16:41.140
And that is
effectively implemented

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:42.490
as an authorized
network, so the nodes

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:44.380
can talk directly as
an authorized network

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:47.390
to your master.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:48.640
Yeah.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.690
Next up is RBAC.

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.660
RBAC is role-based
access control,

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.640
and it's how you set permissions
on individual resources

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:56.750
in Kubernetes.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.460
So things like how
you set permissions

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:03.830
to use pods, deployments,
containers, et cetera.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:07.280
Basically any resource
that you can think of.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:10.490
You define the roles based
on your particular use cases,

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:15.079
so you would say, for example,
I need a human user or a plugin,

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.900
like my logging plug-in, to
talk to a particular component.

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:21.770
You'd give it a role
by binding the user

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:26.150
in green here to the role in
red and the set of permissions

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.407
to that particular role.

00:17:28.407 --> 00:17:30.740
There's also a couple of
predefined roles in Kubernetes,

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:33.114
including things like cluster
admin for common use cases.

00:17:35.432 --> 00:17:36.890
Now the recommendation
that we have

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:40.429
here is that you disable ABAC.

00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:41.470
Sorry, let me chase back.

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.360
RBAC is relatively
new in Kubernetes.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:46.550
RBAC was introduced in 1.6 and
was implemented as the default

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:47.945
starting in 1.8.

00:17:47.945 --> 00:17:49.820
The security recommendation
that we have here

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:51.950
is that you disable
another system called ABAC,

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:53.870
or attribute based
access control,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:57.660
and instead enable RBAC
with minimum permissions.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.600
Now if you're already
using ABAC on premises

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:01.179
and it works for
you, that's great.

00:18:01.179 --> 00:18:02.220
There's no problem there.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.650
You don't have to
change anything.

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:05.774
But what we typically see
with Enterprise customers

00:18:05.774 --> 00:18:09.685
is that they're using a role
based access control mechanism

00:18:09.685 --> 00:18:10.310
anyways, right?

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:11.120
It's easier to manage.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:12.703
If somebody quits
my company tomorrow,

00:18:12.703 --> 00:18:14.390
I can just remove
them from that role,

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.570
rather than having to change
individual permissions.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.280
So if Jesse, for
some reason, decided

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.082
to quit the company he
co-founded tomorrow,

00:18:21.082 --> 00:18:22.790
they could just take
him off of that role

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.600
and he wouldn't have
access to customer data.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.760
Another good use case
for using RBAC here--

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.100
I think about it as
for compliance needs.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:33.530
So if I need to be able
to say, for example, who

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:35.090
has access to customer data?

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:36.980
If I only have one role
that has that access,

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.938
I can just look at who
has access to that role.

00:18:38.938 --> 00:18:41.934
It's much easier for
me to maintain that.

00:18:41.934 --> 00:18:43.600
JESSE ENDAHL: So we
have an example here

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.930
of what a RBAC
definition looks like.

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.760
This is from the
Kubernetes documentation,

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:52.785
and you can see here
highlighted in blue again that--

00:18:52.785 --> 00:18:54.660
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It's
a YAML file with blue?

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:55.535
JESSE ENDAHL: Indeed.

00:18:55.535 --> 00:18:56.740
Indeed.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:58.830
We're giving the
blue team user here

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:02.246
cluster admin rights
in the blue namespace,

00:19:02.246 --> 00:19:03.370
and you can see that there.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:04.870
So in the metadata
section, you've

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:08.200
got the namespace,
subjects, the user account,

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:12.179
and of course down at the
bottom there is the admin role.

00:19:12.179 --> 00:19:13.970
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
on Kubernetes engine,

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:15.080
it's also very similar.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:17.390
So like it is in
Kubernetes open source,

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:20.540
Kubernetes engine enables
RBAC by default for clusters

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.287
created with 1.8 or higher.

00:19:22.287 --> 00:19:24.870
That is, if you open your laptop
right now and go to a new tab

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.050
and create a new
cluster, RBAC's enabled.

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:29.190
You don't have to do anything.

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:31.070
The recommendation
for Kubernetes engine

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:33.410
is that you use IAM
to manage permissions

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:35.090
at the project level.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.490
For example, using the
Kubernetes developer role

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.321
for granting access
to your cluster,

00:19:39.321 --> 00:19:41.570
and then using RBAC at the
cluster and namespace level

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:43.794
to set permissions on
individual resources.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.290
JESSE ENDAHL: Next up, we
have using a network policy.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.800
So as we mentioned
earlier, the default

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.870
for all pod to pod
communication is open.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.770
And so if that
scares you or if you

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:00.530
have reasons to
isolate that traffic,

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:03.770
then you can use the network
policy to accomplish that.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:06.215
And so we have an example
of what that looks like.

00:20:06.215 --> 00:20:08.090
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: One
thing to point out here

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.100
with network policy, because I
often get asked this question,

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:12.476
is that this is really about
intra-cluster communication.

00:20:12.476 --> 00:20:14.850
If you're thinking about
cluster to cluster communication

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.420
or service to service
communication,

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:17.640
this doesn't solve that problem.

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:18.230
You're going to want
to use something

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:19.521
like a service mesh like Istio.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.370
JESSE ENDAHL: So more YAML here.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:26.380
More blue highlights.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:27.880
So you can see here
what we're doing

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:31.780
is we're restricting
the traffic from nginx,

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:33.550
such that the only
traffic allowed

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:37.600
is that that's coming from
the ingress controller

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.240
with a label of
access equals true.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.700
And so anything that doesn't
actually have that label

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:44.140
won't be able to talk to nginx.

00:20:48.214 --> 00:20:50.630
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: In Kubernetes
engine, it's very similar.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.680
You can set a network policy by
using the network policy API.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:55.402
At the moment,
just using Calico.

00:20:55.402 --> 00:20:56.860
All you have to do
is pass a flag--

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.080
this enable network
policy flag, and then you

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:01.180
can set a network
policy as normal,

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:02.612
as you would in Kubernetes.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:07.902
Disabling the
Kubernetes dashboard.

00:21:07.902 --> 00:21:10.110
So some of you might be
familiar with this dashboard.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:12.670
It's the UI that's
part of the open source

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:14.470
version of Kubernetes
that lets you

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:16.240
do things like
manage your cluster,

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.050
create a new deployment, monitor
what's going on, et cetera.

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:22.955
How many of you actually use
the Kubernetes dashboard?

00:21:22.955 --> 00:21:25.620
OK, so maybe like 10% of people.

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:26.640
That sounds about right.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.650
Typically, if you're
using a cloud provider

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:31.060
or you prefer the
command line kubectl--

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:31.980
JESSE ENDAHL: kubectl.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.620
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Then
you are probably not

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:36.980
putting this dashboard
into your environment.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:38.790
The reason that we
mention it is that it's

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.870
been sort of part of
some high profile hacks.

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:45.630
It's made some cameo appearances
in the public eye recently,

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:49.320
so specifically, in
February of 2018,

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.960
Tesla made the news for
having a Kubernetes console--

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:55.440
a Kubernetes dashboard that
was open on the public web.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:56.910
This was sitting
on the public web,

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:59.790
not password protected, and had
access to their cloud service

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:01.290
account credentials.

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:04.290
Hackers found this probably on
Shodan or something like that

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:06.630
and stole the credentials,
logged in, and started mining

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.090
cryptocurrency.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.280
That's not particularly unusual.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:12.900
I think that happens
pretty often.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.152
Tesla's not the only one.

00:22:14.152 --> 00:22:16.110
This also happened to
Weight Watchers and Aviva

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:18.210
in the last couple of months.

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:22.020
Lacework estimates that there
are over 20,000 open Kubernetes

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:24.810
dashboards on the web, of
which 300 have no password

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:26.620
protection, whatsoever.

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:29.160
So this is actually a big deal.

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:31.350
So how do I protect myself?

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.100
JESSE ENDAHL: Well you
have three options.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.880
You can take the most
secure and laziest option--

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:37.260
a rare combo there--

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:39.622
of just not deploying
it in the first place

00:22:39.622 --> 00:22:41.580
if you're running your
own cluster because this

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:42.790
is a separate YAML file.

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:43.620
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Another YAML file?

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:44.660
JESSE ENDAHL: Another YAML file.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.230
And so you can just not deploy
it, and then you'll be secure.

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:47.980
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
I like that option.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:48.860
The lazy option.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:50.193
JESSE ENDAHL: It's pretty great.

00:22:50.193 --> 00:22:52.080
That's the option we take.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:55.650
Option number two is that you
could just keep it deployed

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.070
or choose to deploy it, but with
reduced permissions associated

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:01.152
with the service account.

00:23:01.152 --> 00:23:02.610
But obviously, then
you're probably

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:04.260
going to reduce the
functionality of the dashboard.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:06.301
You won't be able to use
it to do as many things.

00:23:06.301 --> 00:23:07.692
So that's a trade-off there.

00:23:07.692 --> 00:23:09.150
Another option is
that you can just

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:10.858
stick some authentication
in front of it,

00:23:10.858 --> 00:23:13.740
maybe using a reverse proxy that
requires G Suite authentication

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:15.580
or something like that.

00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:17.847
So that's your
option number three.

00:23:17.847 --> 00:23:20.430
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: On Kubernetes
engine, we followed option two

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:21.930
and then kind of
went to option one.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:24.920
So starting with Kubernetes
engine 1.7 or higher,

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:26.910
we've removed permissions
from that dashboard.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:28.844
It doesn't have admin
access by default.

00:23:28.844 --> 00:23:31.010
And starting with Kubernetes
engine 1.10 and higher,

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:32.760
the dashboard is just
completely disabled.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:35.090
We don't deploy it for you.

00:23:35.090 --> 00:23:36.920
If you're using
Kubernetes engine,

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:38.780
you probably can just use the
cloud console for anything

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:40.738
you'd want to do anyways--
or the command line.

00:23:40.738 --> 00:23:42.050
That's what we recommend.

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:44.482
And a quick note here,
though, obviously.

00:23:44.482 --> 00:23:46.190
These are just for
new cluster creations,

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:47.480
so if you have an old
cluster that you've

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.850
been updating for the last
year, please go in and disable

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:50.650
that dashboard, right?

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.275
That's not disabled
for you by default.

00:23:52.275 --> 00:23:54.620
So take care of that to
make sure you are not open.

00:23:56.752 --> 00:23:58.210
JESSE ENDAHL: So
next up, we've got

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:02.500
that famed, infamous default
service account token.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.950
So it's important to
understand what this is.

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.020
This is not GCP
service accounts.

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:09.520
This is Kubernetes
service accounts.

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:13.120
So just to kind of clarify
that before we jump in.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:16.150
Every namespace gets its
own default service account.

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:19.390
It's used by the pod to
assert its identity to the API

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:20.640
controller.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.550
And so if you don't specify a
more specific service account,

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:26.816
the default behavior
is that every pod

00:24:26.816 --> 00:24:28.690
will have this default
service account token.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.714
It'll use it and the
permissions are fairly generous,

00:24:31.714 --> 00:24:33.130
and so the reason
that's dangerous

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:35.140
is because if a
pod is compromised,

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:39.790
an attacker could steal and use
that token to do bad things--

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:41.350
to perform arbitrary operations.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:46.790
And so that's sort of what
we're dealing with here.

00:24:46.790 --> 00:24:50.140
So the nice thing is that it's
disabled by default now, as

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.000
of Kubernetes 1.8 and higher.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:54.490
But if you do have
a legacy cluster

00:24:54.490 --> 00:24:56.950
or if you are just
paranoid and you

00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:59.290
want to make absolutely
sure it's not enabled,

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:04.840
you can define that in the
YAML by just setting auto mount

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.877
service account token to false.

00:25:06.877 --> 00:25:08.960
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Basically,
once RBAC is enabled,

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:11.740
that token still exists,
but it has no permissions.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.040
It can't do anything.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:15.562
So if you turn on RBAC,
you get this for free.

00:25:15.562 --> 00:25:17.770
And that's kind of what
happens in Kubernetes engine.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.187
Since we turned on RBAC
by default, as a reminder,

00:25:20.187 --> 00:25:20.895
ABAC is disabled.

00:25:20.895 --> 00:25:24.300
RBAC is enabled by default for
clusters created since 1.8.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.200
That token doesn't
have any privileges

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:30.211
and can't be abused
in your environment.

00:25:30.211 --> 00:25:32.460
The last thing we'll talk
about is pod security policy

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:34.490
and various Linux
security constructs.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:36.460
So some of these, you
might be familiar with.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:38.880
AppArmor is a Linux
Security Module, or LSM,

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:41.260
that let's you restrict
your program's actions.

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:45.180
Things like file reads,
writes, and executions.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.110
It basically means
that somebody can't

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:49.860
run arbitrary commands
in your environment

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.630
that you might not
want it to run.

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.850
Seccomp, short for
secure computing mode,

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.811
basically filters
the set of sys calls

00:25:55.811 --> 00:25:57.060
that your application can run.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:25:59.910
It puts your application in
a one way, secure state where

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:01.410
if it tries to do
a set of sys calls

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:03.750
that's not allowed-- like
it tries to do something

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:05.970
other than sigreturn
or exit, et cetera,

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:07.710
the process is
automatically killed.

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:09.900
And so it can't execute
in your environment.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.230
There's a pretty good default--

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:12.960
the Docker seccomp
default profile--

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:16.650
that limits about 150 uncommon
or potentially unsafe sys

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:19.320
calls in your environment,
and that's a good place to get

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:22.744
started if you're not sure
what to do in your space.

00:26:22.744 --> 00:26:24.410
So how this is
implemented in Kubernetes

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:26.214
is actually very
confusing, and so we

00:26:26.214 --> 00:26:27.630
wanted to spend
some time on this,

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:30.900
so people make sure
other people understand.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:32.580
If you have these
different things

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.830
you'd like, like AppArmor,
seccomp, et cetera,

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.660
you define a set of
these things that you'd

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:39.210
like to implement in the
security context of your pod

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:40.386
specification.

00:26:40.386 --> 00:26:41.760
This is not a
separate YAML file.

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:44.200
This is an existing
YAML file that you have.

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.290
So your existing pod
specification YAML file--

00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:48.581
you define the security
context for the things we just

00:26:48.581 --> 00:26:49.080
mentioned--

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:50.760
AppArmor, seccomp,
run written on root--

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:53.130
a variety of other things.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:55.662
Just by defining it in
that pod specification,

00:26:55.662 --> 00:26:57.870
it'll be enforced at container
runtime by the runtime

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:00.930
that you're using-- by
Docker or by CRI-O--

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:02.692
by whatever you happen to use.

00:27:02.692 --> 00:27:04.150
JESSE ENDAHL: So
the other option--

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:07.090
if you want to do this
on more than just one pod

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:09.910
specification, rather
than copy, pasting

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:12.760
that across all
your pod YAML files,

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:18.070
you can enforce this globally
by using a pod security policy.

00:27:18.070 --> 00:27:20.360
How that works is it uses
an admission controller,

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:22.210
which is a concept
in Kubernetes that

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.460
kicks in after authentication
and authorization.

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:28.090
It's sort of a third gate,
and it looks at every pod

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:33.070
entering the cluster and decides
whether it shall pass or not.

00:27:33.070 --> 00:27:37.720
And so basically, if you want
kind of a global pod security

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:40.850
policy, you would
want to set it--

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:41.350
sorry.

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:44.772
Security context applied
and enforce that runtime.

00:27:44.772 --> 00:27:47.230
The way you would do that is
by using a pod security policy

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.890
and enabling its associated
admission controller,

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:53.290
which we will cover--

00:27:53.290 --> 00:27:55.750
the very critical step of how
to make sure that actually

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.910
happens on the next
slide, I believe.

00:27:57.910 --> 00:27:59.440
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And just to
clarify here-- the security

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:01.940
context is enforced at runtime,
so once the thing is running

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:03.752
an environment, the
pod security policy

00:28:03.752 --> 00:28:04.960
is enforced at creation time.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:07.430
So when the pod is being
created in your cluster.

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:09.160
So that's why it's
also extra confusing,

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:10.201
I think, to follow along.

00:28:13.484 --> 00:28:14.400
JESSE ENDAHL: So yeah.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:15.937
This is what I was alluding to.

00:28:15.937 --> 00:28:17.520
It's really critical
that you actually

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:20.280
enable the admission controller
plug-in for pod security

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:24.660
policy, because otherwise,
that lovely definition that you

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.979
created will do nothing.

00:28:26.979 --> 00:28:28.770
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
the restricted policy

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.103
is a pretty good starting point.

00:28:30.103 --> 00:28:32.200
Do you want to walk us
through some of this stuff?

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:34.590
JESSE ENDAHL: Yeah,
so basically, if we

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:36.200
go from top to bottom here--

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:37.500
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Blue YAML.

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.450
JESSE ENDAHL: Blue YAML.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:41.130
You can see that we're
limiting the pods

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:44.130
from running as privileged
on the first blue line there.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:47.430
The second, we're preventing
privilege escalation.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:49.230
The third, we're
requiring the container

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:51.690
to actually run without
root privileges.

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:53.190
And fourth, we're
forbidding the pod

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:54.587
from being run as
the root group.

00:28:54.587 --> 00:28:56.920
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And it's a
much longer file than this.

00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:59.340
We just zoomed in on the parts
that are most interesting.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:01.260
You can find it on
the Kubernetes GitHub.

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:06.560
For Kubernetes engine, you can
limit it in much the same way.

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:09.500
You just create a cluster
with a pod security policy

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:12.440
by passing a flag to
enable pod security policy.

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:14.630
Just a quick note on both
Kubernetes and Kubernetes

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:15.320
engine--

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:16.790
if you enable pod
security policy,

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:18.339
you have to pass it a policy.

00:29:18.339 --> 00:29:19.880
Otherwise, it assumes
that the policy

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:21.950
that you passed it
is the empty policy,

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:23.909
and you will have no
pods in your cluster

00:29:23.909 --> 00:29:25.700
because none of them
will match the policy.

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:29.490
So what's next?

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.250
So we've covered
these six items.

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:34.460
So quickly recap what
we talked through.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:37.504
The first one-- restricting
access to kubectl.

00:29:37.504 --> 00:29:38.420
JESSE ENDAHL: kubectl.

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:40.550
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
kubectl by using

00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:43.850
a bastion host for Kubernetes
or using authorized networks

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:45.680
in GKE.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:47.510
Second, using RBAC--
so role-based access

00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:50.400
control to define roles
and their permissions.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.480
So that's enabled by
default since 1.8.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:54.980
If you're running
on GKE, we suggest

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:56.870
you use IAM at the
project level and RBAC

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:58.730
at the cluster namespace level.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:01.452
Using a network policy to
control pod to pod traffic.

00:30:01.452 --> 00:30:03.035
Pod to pod traffic
is open by default,

00:30:03.035 --> 00:30:06.560
so you can restrict that using
a network policy natively

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:08.990
available in GKE, as well.

00:30:08.990 --> 00:30:10.790
Protecting the
Kubernetes dashboard.

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:12.670
Either disable it or
restrict access to it,

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:15.710
given that it uses a highly
privileged service account.

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:17.770
In Kubernetes, it's not
deployed by default,

00:30:17.770 --> 00:30:19.650
so the lazy option
works pretty well.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:23.870
And on Kubernetes engine, it
has no admin access since 1.7

00:30:23.870 --> 00:30:25.640
and is fully
disabled since 1.10.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:29.060
But if you've been upgrading
your cluster, go disable that.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:33.200
Next, disable the
service account token

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:37.290
by enabling RBAC,
which gives that token

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:38.510
no actual permissions.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:42.860
It's default in Kubernetes
and GKE since 1.8.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:46.040
And using a pod security
policy to enable things

00:30:46.040 --> 00:30:48.902
like the Docker seccomp profile
or other security restrictions

00:30:48.902 --> 00:30:51.110
using pod security policy--
the admission controller.

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:55.360
And that's in beta in
both Kubernetes and GKE.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:57.259
So we talked about
some of these things.

00:30:57.259 --> 00:30:59.050
Some of the things that
are grayed out here

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:01.030
now are actually all
the things that GKE

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:02.842
does for you by
default. So you don't

00:31:02.842 --> 00:31:05.050
have to worry about setting
up any of the things that

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:06.110
are grayed out.

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:07.810
I also want to point out that
a lot of the other things

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:09.935
are one line changes we
were talking about earlier.

00:31:09.935 --> 00:31:11.590
Using containers--
using Kubernetes

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:13.852
makes these things
relatively simple.

00:31:13.852 --> 00:31:16.310
Restricting access to kubectl,
turn on authorized networks.

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.850
Network policy-- pass
the network policy.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.202
Set a pod security policy,
pass a pod security policy.

00:31:21.202 --> 00:31:22.660
It's fairly
straightforward for you

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:23.951
to enable some of these things.

00:31:25.935 --> 00:31:27.310
And I also want
to highlight some

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:29.643
of the areas that are a little
bit newer in this space--

00:31:29.643 --> 00:31:32.441
newer ideas and concepts that
are coming to discussion.

00:31:32.441 --> 00:31:34.440
One of these here is
verifying that the binaries

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:38.312
that you've built are deployed.

00:31:38.312 --> 00:31:40.270
There's a couple of open
source projects here--

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.250
Kritis and Grafeas--
that you might

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:44.830
have seen from Google come out
in December around how we make

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:46.960
sure that what is deployed
into your environment

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:49.240
is actually what you
built-- what you've signed.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:50.990
Scanning images for
known vulnerabilities.

00:31:50.990 --> 00:31:52.404
You can use a partner solution.

00:31:52.404 --> 00:31:54.820
There's open source solutions
like Clair and Micro Scanner

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:57.160
to make sure that you know
what's actually in images

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:58.930
before you deploy them.

00:31:58.930 --> 00:32:01.000
And then further down
the line, sandboxing--

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.200
Google open-source
gVisor in May.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.140
There's a talk on
gVisor later this week.

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:06.520
Do check it out.

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:08.610
Limiting the identity
that are used by pods.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:10.480
There's a new proposal
around pod identity

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:12.302
in Kubernetes open source.

00:32:12.302 --> 00:32:14.510
So if you're interested,
please do go check that out.

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:16.570
Check out the
GitHub issue tracker

00:32:16.570 --> 00:32:19.270
and tell us what you
think of that proposal.

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:21.750
And using a service mesh for
authentication and encryption.

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:24.250
So Istio-- there's lots of talks
at this conference on Istio

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:26.540
and you saw the
announcements this morning.

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:28.819
So check that out, as well.

00:32:28.819 --> 00:32:31.360
So we've been talking a little
bit about Kubernetes hardening

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.340
and GKE hardening.

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:35.880
Hopefully, I'm starting to
convince Jesse and convince

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:39.200
some of you that GKE makes a
lot of that easier for you.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.350
So first off, GKE provides
a lot of the security

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:43.810
best practices by default.
You saw all the things

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:45.280
that were already grayed out.

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:47.572
We're definitely working on
having more and more things

00:32:47.572 --> 00:32:49.030
built in by default
for you, so you

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:50.879
don't have to manage
those from the get go.

00:32:50.879 --> 00:32:53.170
There's always going to be
app-level hardening and fine

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:55.300
tuning to do, and
protecting that top layer--

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:57.970
your actual application in the
shared responsibility model

00:32:57.970 --> 00:33:00.520
is still your responsibility.

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:03.850
Next, GKE patches you to the
latest version automatically,

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:06.410
so Kubernetes engine
manages the master for you

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:08.330
and we patch the master
for you automatically.

00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:10.413
You can also turn on this
feature called node auto

00:33:10.413 --> 00:33:13.150
upgrade, and we'll patch
your nodes for you, as well.

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:15.722
That's a super cool,
security super power feature.

00:33:15.722 --> 00:33:17.680
People don't realize
you'll just get the screen

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.450
patches without you
having to do anything.

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:21.170
That's why you guys went to
containers in the first place,

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:21.670
right?

00:33:21.670 --> 00:33:23.456
You don't have to
manage that stuff.

00:33:23.456 --> 00:33:24.830
So we'll take care
of it for you.

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:27.340
And the last is that
Kubernetes engine plugs

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:28.720
into a lot of the
Google Security

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.844
features that we already
have that exist elsewhere.

00:33:30.844 --> 00:33:33.664
So if you're already familiar
with IAM, Cloud Audit Logging,

00:33:33.664 --> 00:33:35.080
Virtual Private
Clouds, et cetera,

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.630
you can just use those things
with Kubernetes engine.

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:41.650
You don't have to worry
about learning a new concept.

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:45.130
We also have a ton of partners
on GKE and on GCP in general,

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:48.090
including some who are
on container security,

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:48.657
specifically.

00:33:48.657 --> 00:33:50.490
So if that's something
you're interested in,

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:51.250
go check them out.

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:54.740
Go check out the booths that are
here at the conference today.

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:58.750
To learn more about Kubernetes
security and Kubernetes

00:33:58.750 --> 00:34:01.210
engine hardening, check
out the short links.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:05.084
I'll just pause here for a
second and let you take photos.

00:34:05.084 --> 00:34:06.284
OK.

00:34:06.284 --> 00:34:07.700
And then if you
want to learn more

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:09.560
on container security
at this conference,

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:11.330
we have a ton of talks.

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:14.060
So we're the first upper
left hand box here,

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:17.510
but there's a talk later today
on Istio and a bunch tomorrow

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:19.850
and on Thursday to learn more
about container security.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.300
